# Daytona provider for Sandbox Kit

Optional package over `@daytona/sdk@0.224.0`. Use it through `@sandbox-kit/sandbox`.

## Usage

```ts
import { Client } from "@sandbox-kit/sandbox";
import { daytona } from "@sandbox-kit/provider-daytona";

const client = new Client({ provider: daytona() });

try {
  const sandbox = await client.create({
    runtime: { language: "typescript" },
  });
  console.log(sandbox.id);
} finally {
  await client.close();
}
```

The native Daytona SDK resolves credentials from its environment when `auth` is
omitted. Closing the client releases local SDK resources only.

## Examples

| Example    | Project                                                                                          |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Creation   | [create-daytona-sandbox](../../../../examples/typescript/daytona/create-daytona-sandbox/README.md) |
| Policies   | [create-daytona-with-policies](../../../../examples/typescript/daytona/create-daytona-with-policies/README.md) |
| Retrieval  | [get-daytona-sandbox](../../../../examples/typescript/daytona/get-daytona-sandbox/README.md)     |
| Full index | [TypeScript examples](../../../../examples/typescript/README.md)                                 |

## Implementation notes

Checks and portable field mappings are handwritten in `provider_checks.ts`,
`provider_mappings.ts`, and `provider_config.ts`, kept in sync with
`specs/providers/daytona.yaml`. `src/daytona.ts` owns typed Daytona SDK call
ordering and lifecycle orchestration; `src/mappings.ts` owns remaining
provider-specific composition.

## Dependency audit

As of 2026-10-14, `npm audit` reports four high-severity findings in the pinned
`@daytona/sdk@0.224.0` dependency chain (`fast-glob` → `micromatch` → `braces`).
The registry currently reports no fix. The provider does not override those
transitive packages because doing so could create an unsupported SDK
combination; reassess the audit when upgrading the Daytona SDK.

# Modal provider for Sandbox Kit

Optional package over `modal@0.11.0`. Use it through `@sandbox-kit/sandbox`.

## Usage

```ts
import { modal } from "@sandbox-kit/provider-modal";
import { Client } from "@sandbox-kit/sandbox";

await using client = new Client({
  provider: modal(),
  auth: {
    credentials: { case: "tokenPair", value: { id: "...", secret: "..." } },
  },
  scope: { appName: "my-app", environment: "main" },
});

const sandbox = await client.create({
  source: { kind: { case: "image", value: { reference: "alpine:3.21" } } },
});
console.log(sandbox.id);
```

Closing the client releases local SDK resources only. The Modal app must already
exist.

## Examples

| Example    | Project                                                                                        |
| ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Creation   | [create-modal-sandbox](../../../../examples/typescript/modal/create-modal-sandbox/README.md)   |
| Resources  | [create-modal-with-resources](../../../../examples/typescript/modal/create-modal-with-resources/README.md) |
| Attach     | [attach-modal-sandbox](../../../../examples/typescript/modal/attach-modal-sandbox/README.md)   |
| Full index | [TypeScript examples](../../../../examples/typescript/README.md)                               |

## Capabilities

Modal supports image creation, attach, terminate, and local handle detachment.
The creation adapter covers runtime command/workdir/PTY, gVisor or VM isolation,
CPU and memory reservations and limits, placement, network policy and ports,
secrets, OIDC workload identity, readiness, timeouts, labels, and verbose mode.
Unsupported shared intent is rejected before contacting Modal.

## Implementation notes

Checks and portable field mappings are handwritten in `provider_checks.ts`,
`provider_mappings.ts`, and `provider_config.ts`, kept in sync with
`specs/providers/modal.yaml`. `src/modal.ts` owns SDK call ordering;
`src/mappings.ts` owns remaining composition those helpers do not cover.

# Sandbox Kit for TypeScript

Public client for Sandbox Kit. Closing the client releases local provider SDK
resources only; it does not stop or delete cloud sandboxes.

## Usage

```ts
import { Client } from "@sandbox-kit/sandbox";

const client = new Client(config);

try {
  const sandbox = await client.create();
  console.log(sandbox.id);
} finally {
  await client.close();
}
```

With TypeScript 5.2+, `ESNext.Disposable`, and a runtime that supports explicit
resource management:

```ts
await using client = new Client(config);
const sandbox = await client.create();
```

Both forms have identical cleanup semantics.
`client[Symbol.asyncDispose]()` runs automatically at the end of an `await using`
scope.

## Examples

| Language   | Index                                              |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| TypeScript | [examples/typescript](../../../examples/typescript/README.md) |
| Go         | [examples/go](../../../examples/go/README.md)       |

Install a provider package such as `@sandbox-kit/provider-daytona` or
`@sandbox-kit/provider-modal` beside this client. See the
[root README](../../../README.md) for a full create snippet.
